An **enlarged prostate**, also called **benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)**, is when the **prostate gland** gets bigger. It is very common in men and people with a prostate over 50. The prostate sits below the bladder and wraps around the tube that carries pee (the urethra), so when it grows it can squeeze the tube.

## Symptoms

- difficulty **starting to pee**
- a **weak flow**, or stopping and starting
- needing to pee **more often**, or **urgently**
- getting up in the **night** to pee
- feeling your bladder has not emptied properly
- dribbling after you finish

## Getting diagnosed

Your GP may:

- ask you to keep a **bladder diary**
- check your urine for infection
- examine your prostate through your bottom (a rectal examination)
- offer a **PSA blood test**, after talking through the pros and cons
- use a symptom score questionnaire

{% callout type="danger" title="Get urgent help" %}
Go to **A&E** or call **999** if you **cannot pee at all**, especially if your tummy is painful and swollen. This is called **acute urinary retention** and needs a catheter to drain your bladder.
{% /callout %}

Read about [treatment for an enlarged prostate](/conditions/kidney-bladder-and-reproductive/enlarged-prostate/treatment/).
